WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.309
Choosing among Claude, GPT, Gemini, Grok, and open models.

2
00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:09.624
Studio 1 (lesson 3) surveyed what each provider documents as its focus.

3
00:00:09.904 --> 00:00:18.971
For a switching decision, turn that survey into hard constraints first, because they remove candidates before any eval runs: Data and deployment.

4
00:00:19.251 --> 00:00:21.597
Where may your data go, and under what terms?

5
00:00:21.877 --> 00:00:32.736
If it can’t leave your infrastructure, the candidates are open-weight models you host (for example Llama, Qwen, gpt-oss, or Mistral’s open-weight models; read each license).

6
00:00:33.016 --> 00:00:36.899
If you need a specific cloud, check which providers’ models it offers.

7
00:00:37.179 --> 00:00:38.693
Features the task needs.

8
00:00:38.973 --> 00:00:48.765
Structured outputs with the schema features you use, tool use, long context, multimodal inputs, search or real-time data tools, batch processing.

9
00:00:49.045 --> 00:00:52.715
Check each provider’s current docs; they change with every release.

10
00:00:52.995 --> 00:00:54.360
Operational limits.

11
00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:01.787
Rate limits at your volume, regional availability, and the provider’s deprecation schedule for the model you’d adopt.

12
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:03.901
Then run the eval on what’s left.

13
00:01:04.181 --> 00:01:19.307
Use at least one configuration from each serious contender at two effort levels, plus your incumbent and one cheaper tier of the incumbent’s family, because the strongest alternative to switching is often a smaller model from the provider you already use.

14
00:01:19.587 --> 00:01:27.629
Analyze with paired statistics against the incumbent, compute cost per success and p95 latency, and draw the frontier.

15
00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:30.277
A diversity argument also applies.

16
00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:39.432
If you use a model as a judge or checker (week 2), a judge from a different model family than the generator shares fewer of its blind spots.

17
00:01:39.712 --> 00:01:48.096
A multi-provider setup has an operational benefit too: when one provider has an outage or deprecates a model, you have a tested fallback.

18
00:01:48.376 --> 00:01:54.371
Both are reasons to keep a second provider’s configuration in your eval suite even when you don’t switch.
